iiod: zero-copy sends of IIO DMA blocks; usable per connection with -i

 - When the buffer data lies in an mmapped IIO block (high-speed local
   backend) and the client is a socket, send it with vmsplice() + splice()
   instead of write(): no CPU copy (needs patches/linux 0001, which keeps a
   block away from the DMA until the network stack releases its pages; on
   other kernels vmsplice() fails and iiod falls back to write()).
   splice() raises SIGPIPE on a closed socket, and iiod's handler would stop
   the whole server, so it is blocked and discarded around the send.
 - "iiod -i" with sockets as stdin/stdout (omnicored starts one per
   connection) uses the socket code paths. Before, the byte-wise non-socket
   read_line() looped forever at end of stream instead of exiting.
 - With one iiod per connection, a second client opening a device that
   another iiod streams from must fail without touching it: libiio disables
   the buffer through sysfs before it finds the device node busy. Check the
   node first and answer EBUSY.

